 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.nacionnacionalismo.03.focus",
-  "title": "Subjuntivo tras expresiones de necesidad y valoración",
+  "title": "The subjunctive after value judgments: what matters",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "Expressions like *era necesario que*, *era importante que*, *no era extraño que* and *convenía que* all evaluate a fact rather than simply report it — and any time the main clause evaluates instead of asserting, the verb that follows que goes into the subjunctive. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "title": "What you already know",
+      "content": "You know the subjunctive after value judgments from A2 Unit 27: *es importante que*, *es necesario que*, *es injusto que* + present subjunctive. Historians use it to say what matters when a country builds an identity."
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "table",
+      "title": "Value judgment + que + subjunctive",
+      "rows": [
+        [
+          "Es importante que se estudie la vida real.",
+          "*es importante que* + *se estudie*"
+        ],
+        [
+          "Es necesario que un país explique su pasado.",
+          "*es necesario que* + *explique*"
+        ],
+        [
+          "Es injusto que se olvide a las personas.",
+          "*es injusto que* + *se olvide*"
+        ]
+      ]
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
+      "title": "In context",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "Era necesario que la población reconociera esos símbolos como propios.",
-          "english": "It was necessary for the population to recognize those symbols as their own."
+          "spanish": "Es importante que se estudien los símbolos de la nación.",
