 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.nacionnacionalismo.04.focus",
-  "title": "Contraste y concesión: aunque, mientras que, sin embargo",
+  "title": "Aunque: borders on the map, life on the ground",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "Aunque admits a fact and immediately qualifies it within the same sentence; mientras que sets two facts side by side as a direct contrast; *sin embargo* opens a new sentence that pushes back against what came before. All three let a text hold an official claim and a messier reality in view at once. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "title": "What you already know",
+      "content": "You know concessive clauses with *aunque* from A2 Unit 28. They join two ideas that seem to pull against each other. In this lesson the contrast is between what an official map or a state claimed and what people really did."
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "table",
+      "title": "Aunque + indicative",
+      "rows": [
+        [
+          "Aunque los mapas mostraban una línea, las personas la cruzaban.",
+          "the map says one thing, people do another"
+        ],
+        [
+          "Aunque Paraguay luchó durante años, perdió la guerra.",
+          "the effort did not change the result"
+        ],
+        [
+          "La idea oficial era clara, aunque la realidad era más complicada.",
+          "*aunque* can also come in the middle"
+        ]
+      ]
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
+      "title": "In context",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "El Estado presentaba el territorio como una unidad natural, aunque en la práctica existían pueblos cuya vida ignoraba esas líneas.",
-          "english": "The state presented the territory as a natural unity, although in practice there were peoples whose lives ignored those lines."
+          "spanish": "Aunque había una frontera, muchas familias seguían juntas.",
+          "english": "Although there was a border, many families stayed together."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Mientras que las fronteras modernas fueron trazadas por acuerdos diplomáticos, las comunidades aymaras habían mantenido redes que cruzaban esas zonas.",
-          "english": "While modern borders were drawn by diplomatic agreements, Aymara communities had maintained networks that crossed those areas."
+          "spanish": "Aunque Bolivia perdió la costa, siguió siendo un país.",
+          "english": "Although Bolivia lost its coast, it stayed a country."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "El Estado, sin embargo, insistía en presentar a sus ciudadanos como una comunidad nacional homogénea.",
-          "english": "The state, however, insisted on presenting its citizens as a homogeneous national community."
+          "spanish": "Los mapas mostraban una línea, aunque la vida seguía igual.",
+          "english": "The maps showed a line, although life went on as before."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Los aymaras continuaron practicando vínculos transfronterizos, aunque las autoridades los reconocieran cada vez menos.",
-          "english": "The Aymara kept practicing cross-border ties, even though the authorities recognized them less and less."
+          "spanish": "Aunque las escuelas enseñaban símbolos nacionales, las familias hablaban su idioma.",
+          "english": "Although the schools taught national symbols, families spoke their own language."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "Aunque + subjunctive (aunque las autoridades... reconocieran) signals that the concession is about an ongoing attitude, not a settled fact — compare it with aunque + indicative, which would present the concession as simply true. Both are common; the subjunctive version here softens it slightly toward 'even granting that...'"
+      "content": "After *aunque* use the indicative for something that is a fact (*aunque había una frontera*). *Aunque* can start the sentence (with a comma after the first part) or come after the main clause. *A pesar de que* means almost the same and is a little more formal."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-nacionnacionalismo-05-focus-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-nacionnacionalismo-05-focus-gr.json
